                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Drive!Drive!Drive! is a 'racing game unlike any other', the store page proudly proclaims. They're right; no other racing game will 'Drive' you insane quite like this one.\n\nThe basic idea is easy enough to understand: Try racing multiple Hot Wheels-like tracks at once, obviously driving one car at a time and switching to another lane when that car falls behind or when you need to collect crystals in the Collection events.\n\nThe problems start to add up once you realize that Drive!Drive!Drive! is constantly working against you, and I don't just mean because of the 'Intelligent Stupidity' engine. (Again, the store page's words, not mine.) When you're racing on one lane, your cars on the other lanes will slow down deliberately to create some sort of difficulty. The idea is that you switch when you see a car fall behind and race to catch back up.\n\nWhere this idea falls apart is more often than not, you'll switch to find your car stuck on another lane it's not supposed to be on, or stuck in a corner not going anywhere other than the inside of the corner it's smashing into, or just plain getting sabotaged by the other AI cars. There is a Reset Car button, but this honestly feels like a cop-out feature to me, like in playtesting these issues came up and they just said \"Oh, well if they can reset whenever it's not a problem as long as we put them in front of the obstacle a little bit, right?\" Well, that sometimes doesn't work right and you'll respawn only to immediately fall off course again. In one case, I just fell through the road entirely upon respawning, so that's fun.\n\nGuess what else is broken in this game? The physics, the one thing an arcade racing game needs to get mostly right if not 100% right, and this game's physics are all sorts of wrong. Go airborne, land on your wheels, and sometimes you'll just dead stop. Yep, makes sense to me. [spoiler]It doesn't.[/spoiler] Try spinning another racer with your car or truck, like you'll have to do if you want to win the Arcade events, and get ready to fly into a tailspin yourself and most likely fly off course. I can't think of a single other arcade racing game where touching another car, even slightly, can completely ruin an entire race. It's just bad design, period.\n\nSpeaking of Arcade events, winning on all lanes in an Arcade event isn't even close to achieving BRONZE. You know what counts the most? Smashes. So your best bet is to pick a big truck and smash the other racers instead of trying to win. I don't know, that doesn't sound like a racing game to me. Fortunately, once you figure out how to get Bronze in the Arcade events (and GOOD LUCK getting above that, you're gonna need it, even on Normal difficulty), the other game modes are just as easy to achieve Bronze. That's all you need to do to progress in the main campaign, which is great because of how luck based these events feel at all times. Then you get to the Offensive campaign, realize all of the races there require absolute perfection, and you'll probably do what I did: stop playing entirely and get a refund.\n\nNormally, I don't like the idea of bashing on games like this, because Drive!Drive!Drive! has some interesting ideas going for it, but I had to write this after seeing the positive reviews. As someone that was able to complete F-Zero GX's story mode on Very Hard difficulty, Drive!Drive!Drive! doesn't feel challenging in a fair, rational way. It's like the racing game equivalent of playing blackjack with a card dealer that wants you to lose, so he rigs the deck against you and occasionally punches you in the face.\n\n[b]Recommendation:[/b] I honestly feel like this is a racing game for people that hate racing games. When half of your game relies on pure luck against bad physics and insurmountable win conditions to experience [u]half of the game's content,[/u] I just can't recommend it to anyone unless I actually hated them with a passion. Instead of this, I recommend playing any of the TrackMania games, probably Nations Forever if I had to pick one. At least that one's free.",
